Question: 
Another legal XML tag not used in this lab is the "stand-alone" tag. 
This kind of tag combines both a start-tag and end-tag in one. It is 
identified with a '/'(slash) preceding the final >. (For example, the 
<foo/> is a stand-alone tag that is "self balancing".
Describe briefly how you would modify Requirement 1 to allow this kind 
of tag. 

Answer:
To support the stand alone XML tags, I should modify my algorithm to 
take it into account. When reading a tag, if it is detected that the tag 
ends with />, it should be treated as self-balancing. This means that 
no push or pop operation should be performed on the stack. The algorithm 
would simply move onto processing the next input. This means that 
stand-along tags will not affect the stack and will not cause any 
mismatches when validating XML structure. 

Specifically, in this section of code where the reading of the contents 
after < and/or </ happens:

while (isalpha(ch)) { 
			tag[i++] = ch; 
			ch = getchar(); 
		}
		tag[i] = '\0'; //marks the end of the string

		if (isEnd) { //non 0 to be true
			char *topTag = pop(); 
			if (topTag == NULL || strcmp(topTag, tag) != 0) { 
			printf("Invalid XML\n");
			exit(0);
			}
		} else {
		char *copy = malloc(strlen(tag) + 1); 
		strcpy(copy, tag); 
		push(copy); 
		}
	}
	
I can add a section where it checks the next char after all the 
alphabetical letters are checked, then have a conditional statement 
where it skips through all the stack operations. 


